# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 5 e), with its solution sheet.
id: sum-up-to-n
title: The sum 0 + 1 + ... + n
topic: Loops
level: 3
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 e
tags: while, invariant, sum, case analysis

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. The postcondition
  $x = 0 + 1 + ... + n$ is written $x = sum(k, 0, n, k)$. When $n$ is
  negative the sum has no term, and its value is $0$.

  Hint from the exercise list: fill a table with the values of $x$ and $y$
  after each iteration, without evaluating the additions.

pre: x = 0 && y = 0
post: x = sum(k, 0, n, k)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (y < n) do {
    {I && y < n}
    {R2}
    y := y + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    x := x + y       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(y < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Trace the loop
rule: LOGIC
text:
  | Iteration | $y$ | $x$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | 0 | $0$ | $0$ |
  | 1 | $1$ | $0 + 1$ |
  | 2 | $2$ | $0 + 1 + 2$ |
  | 3 | $3$ | $0 + 1 + 2 + 3$ |

  At every loop head $x = 0 + 1 + ... + y$, that is $x = sum(k, 0, y, k)$.
  When $n > 0$ the variable $y$ goes from $0$ up to $n$. When $n <= 0$ the
  loop does not run, and $x$ and $y$ stay $0$.

step: The invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The solution sheet separates the two cases of $n$. We add the bound
  $0 <= y$ in the case $n > 0$: without it, preservation fails on states
  with a negative $y$, such as $y = -5$, $x = 0$, $n = 3$, which satisfy
  $x = sum(k, 0, y, k)$ because the sum is empty.
set: I = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x = 0 && y = 0)

step: Establishment
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = 0 && y = 0 ==> I
why:
  If $n > 0$: $0 <= 0 <= n$, and $sum(k, 0, 0, k) = 0$. If $n <= 0$: the
  precondition gives $x = 0 && y = 0$ directly.

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(x := x + y, I)
  = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x + y = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + y = 0 && y = 0)
set: R1 = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y <= n && x + y = sum(k, 0, y, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + y = 0 && y = 0)
calc:
  wp(y := y + 1, R1)
  = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y + 1 <= n && x + (y + 1) = sum(k, 0, y + 1, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + (y + 1) = 0 && y + 1 = 0)
set: R2 = (n > 0 ==> 0 <= y + 1 <= n && x + (y + 1) = sum(k, 0, y + 1, k)) && (n <= 0 ==> x + (y + 1) = 0 && y + 1 = 0)
vc: I && y < n ==> R2
why:
  Case $n > 0$: from $0 <= y < n$ we get $0 <= y + 1 <= n$. Since $y >= 0$,
  $sum(k, 0, y + 1, k) = sum(k, 0, y, k) + (y + 1)$, which is $x + (y + 1)$.

  Case $n <= 0$: the invariant gives $y = 0$, and the condition gives
  $y < n <= 0$. These contradict each other, so the case is impossible and
  the implication holds.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: I && !(y < n) ==> x = sum(k, 0, n, k)
why:
  Case $n > 0$: $y <= n$ and $y >= n$ give $y = n$. Case $n <= 0$: $x = 0$,
  and $sum(k, 0, n, k) = 0$, since for $n = 0$ the only term is $0$ and for
  $n < 0$ there is no term.
result: The triple is valid. A variant for termination is $n - y$.
